M|BADLANDS
O|
M|by Terence Malick
O|
M|Final Version
M|Dialogue and Continuity
O|
O|
O|
T|FADE IN
O|
O|
S|INT. BEDROOM 
O|
N,I|The time is 1959. HOLLY SARGIS, the 15-year-old heroine, sits on 
N,G|her bed, petting her dog. The CAMERA DOLLIES BACK as she talks on 
N|VOICE OVER. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|My mother died of pneumonia when I was just a kid. My father had 
U|kept their wedding cake in the freezer for ten whole years. After 
U|the funeral he gave it to the yardman... He tried to act 
U|cheerful, but he could never be consoled by the little stranger 
U|he found in his house. Then, one day, hoping to begin a new life 
U|away from the scene of all his memories, he moved us from Texas 
U|to Ft. Dupree, South Dakota.
O|
N,G|HOLD on FULL SHOT of bedroom. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S,G|EXT. ALLEYS AND STREETS - SERIES OF ANGLES - DAWN 
O|
N|The streets and alleys of Ft. Dupree at dawn. On sound we hear 
N|the clucking of DOVES. A garbage truck appears. Details of the 
N|mechanisms at the back of the truck. 
O|
N,G|NEW ANGLE 
O|
N,I|KIT CARRUTHERS, the hero, a 25-year-old garbageman, kneels beside 
N,I|a dead dog. He inspects it briefly. then looks back at his friend 
N,I|and co-worker, CATO, a stocky man in his forties. 
O|
C|KIT
U|I'll give you a dollar to eat this collie.
O|
N|Cato inspects the dog. 
O|
C|CATO
U|I'm not going to eat him for a dollar... I don't think he's a 
U|collie, either. Some kind of dog.
O|
N|They drive off. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Watch your heads.
O|
N,G|NEW ANGLE 
O|
N|The truck comes to a stop. Kit bangs on the driver's door. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey. Woody. Gimme a cigarette.
O|
N|WOODY waves him off. Kit shrugs to Cato. 
O|
C|KIT
U,P|Woody wouldn't give me a cigarette. (pause) Ever notice he don't 
U|talk much?
O|
N|Cato agrees with this. They make a terrible racket, with no 
N|regard for the sleep of the neighbors. 
O|
S|EXT. STREET 
O|
N|Holly, whispering some rhyme to herself, twirls a baton in the 
N|middle of an empty street. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Little did I realize that what began in the alleys and back ways 
U|of this quiet town would end in the Badlands of Montana.
O|
S|EXT. ALLEY 
O|
N|Kit tries to sell a passing BUM a pair of shoes. 
O|
C|BUM
U|Nah. they wouldn't fit.
O|
C|KIT
U|How do you know? You hadn't tried them on yet.
O|
C|BUM
U|Nah.
O|
C|KIT
U|Gimme a dollar for them... Cost twenty new.
O|
N|The Bum walks off. Kit pitches the shoes to Cato. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Why don't you see if they fit you?
O|
N|Cato picks them up and looks at them. 
O|
N,G|CLOSE ON TRASH CAN 
O|
N|Kit is culling through a trash can, looking for valuables. 
N|reading other people's mail, etc. 
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|This lady don't ever pay her bills. She's gonna get in trouble if 
U|she doesn't watch out.
O|
N|Cato, ignoring him, picks up a magazine that is lying in the 
N,G|grass. When the CAMERA returns to Kit, he has stripped off his 
N|apron. 
O|
C|KIT
U|I throwed enough trash for today, Cato.... I'll see you In the 
U|morning.
O|
N|He slaps Cato on the back and walks off. Cato throws a mouldy 
N|loaf of bread at his back. 
O|
C|CATO
U|Catch!
O|
C|KIT
U|What do you mean?
O|
N|He throws the loaf back at Cato. 
O|
S|EXT. ALLEYS 
O|
N|Kit walks through the deserted alleys of the sleeping town... as 
N,M|the MAIN TITLES APPEAR. He balances a stolen mop on his finger; 
N|he stomps a can and looks around to see if anyone has spotted him 
N,M|at this. As the CREDITS end he sees Holly in front of her house 
N|twirling her baton. He crosses the street and introduces himself. 
O|
S|EXT. FRONT LAWN 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi, I'm Kit. I'm not keeping you from anything important, am I?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|No.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I was just messing around over there, thought I'd come over 
U,P|and say hello to you. (smiling) I'll try anything once. (pause) 
U|What's your name? I said mine.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Holly.
O|
C|KIT
U|Listen, Holly. you want to take a walk with me?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What for?
O|
C|KIT
U|Well. I got some stuff to say. Guess I'm kind of lucky that way. 
U|Most people don't have anything on their minds, do they?
O|
N|Holly eyes him suspiciously. 
O|
S|EXT. STREET 
O|
N|They walk down the middle of the street. Holly has accepted his 
N|offer. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Oh, incidentally, my last name is Carruthers. Sounds a little too 
U|much like druthers, doesn't it?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|It's okay.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, nobody asked me what I thought. They just hung it on me.
O|
N|Holly breaks the silence that follows. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|You still in school?
O|
C|KIT
U|Nah, I got me a job.
O|
C,E|HOLLY (o.c.)
U|Doing what? 
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I don't mind getting up early, so I got a job throwing 
U|garbage... I'm not in love with the stuff, okay.
O|
N|In the distance we hear Holly's FATHER calling her. She eases 
N|off. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|That's my father. I got to run.
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey. wait a minute. When am I going to see you again?
O|
N|Holly isn't sure how to reply. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, I know what my daddy's going to say.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|What?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Can I be honest?
O|
C|KIT
U|Sure.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, that I shouldn't be seen with anybody that collects 
U|garbage.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|He'll say that?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yeah.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|Now what's he know about garbage, huh?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Nothing.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|There you go.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, I mean there's nothing he wants to know about it... I've 
U|got to run.
O|
N|She waves goodbye and runs off. Kit waves back. 
O|
S|EXT. HOLLY'S BACK YARD 
O|
N|Holly walks into the back yard, where her father is working. 
N|Half-painted signs lean against the garage. 
O|
C|FATHER
U|Who was that?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Just some boy.
O|
N|He throws some paint off his brush onto the ground. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Is that going to kill the grass?
O|
N|He flicks his brush teasingly at her feet. She retreats to a 
N|chair, giggling. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|You got some on my feet.
O|
C|FATHER
U|What'd he want?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Just wanted to know if we needed any yardwork done... I said no, 
U|and for him not to bother us.
O|
S|INT. EMPLOYMENT AGENCY 
O|
N|Kit is talking with a CLERK inside an employment agency. 
O|
C,E|CLERK (o.c.)
U|You ever held another job before?
O|
C|KIT
U|I used to throw trash for the City.
O|
C|CLERK
U|You lost that one?
O|
C|KIT
U|Wouldn't be here if I hadn't.
O|
C|CLERK
U|What kind of work do you think you would be qualified for? 
O|
C|KIT
U|I can't think of anything at the moment... I'd like you to write 
U|me out a slip, though, proving I came down here.
O|
N|The Clerk leafs through his papers. 
O|
C|CLERK
U|Well, I think we could find you something... working cattle over 
U|at the pens.
O|
N|Kit sighs. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Well. what the hell. Just hope there's a breeze.
O|
N|The Clerk gives him a slip of paper. 
O|
S|EXT. FRONT PORCH OF HOLLY'S HOUSE 
O|
N|Holly sees Kit approaching the house. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|He was handsomer than anybody I'd ever met. He looked just like 
U|James Dean.
O|
N|She walks out the front door. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, stop the world.
O|
C|KIT
U|Quit my job.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Great.
O|
C|KIT
U|Just seemed like the right move... Whatcha doing?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Spanish.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|How do you say "Quit my Job" in Spanish?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Something mi trabajo.
O|
N|Kit offers her a cigarette, which she declines. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah, well, I'm going to work as a cowboy now... Or thinking 
U|about it. It's a routine, like anything. What do you think?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I don't know.
O|
N|He pulls himself up onto the bannister beside her. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, at least nobody could get on me about wearing these boots 
U|any more.
O|
N|She watches him intently. This makes him uncomfortable. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You want to go for a ride?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, I got homework.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|Bring it along.
O|
N|His forwardness surprises her. 
O|
S|EXT. DOWNTOWN STREET IN FT. DUPREE 
O|
N|They are walking through the downtown area. Kit kicks a paper bag 
N|that someone has discarded on the sidewalk. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Somebody dropped a bag on the sidewalk.
O|
N|She appears nervous about being seen alone with him. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Everybody did that, the whole town'd be a mess ... Hi.
O|
N|He greets a passing stranger. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You're a redhead.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I know.
O|
C|KIT
U|Anybody ever call you "Red"?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yeah, but I don't like it.
O|
C|KIT
U|Why not?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Just don't... I've got a headache.
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah?
O|
N|He steers her toward his car, a customized Mercury. As she gets 
N|in, he asks her
O|
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|Can I come around and see you tomorrow?
O|
C,P|HOLLY (startled)
U|Okay.
O|
S,G|EXT. FEEDLOT - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N,G|We see Kit at work at the feedlot. throwing hay to the cattle, 
N|feeding them pills, etc. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit went to work in the feedlot while I carried on with my 
U|studies. Little by little we fell in love... As I'd never been 
U|popular in school and didn't have a lot of personality, I was 
U|surprised that he took such a liking to me, especially when he 
U|could've had any other girl in town if he'd given it half a try.
O|
N,G|The MONTAGE includes a shot of Holly running toward Kit's car for 
N|some rendezvous. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|He said that I was grand, though, that he wasn't interested in me 
U|for sex and that coming from him this was a compliment. Held 
U|never met a fifteen-year-old girl who behaved more like a grownup 
U|and wasn't giggly. He didn't care what anybody else thought. I 
U|looked good to him, and whatever I did was okay, and if I didn't 
U|have a lot to say, well, that was okay, too.
O|
S|EXT. TREE BESIDE RIVER 
O|
N|Kit and Holly are sitting under a tree. playing cards on a 
N|blanket. Holly surveys the area. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What a nice place.
O|
C,P|KIT (absorbed in game)
U|Yeah, the tree makes it nice.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|And the flowers... Let's not pick them. They're so nice.
O|
C|KIT
U|It's your play.
O|
N,G|SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|Holly's father paints a picture. using a stereopticon to help 
N,G|with his backgrounds. TRACK with Kit driving down a street in his 
N|Mercury. Holly sits watching football practice. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Of course I had to keep all this a secret from my dad. He 
U|would've had a fit, since Kit was ten years older than me and 
U|came from the wrong side of the tracks, so called. Our time with 
U|each other was limited and each lived for the precious hours when 
U|he or she could be with the other away from all the cares of the 
U|world.
O|
S|EXT. UNDERNEATH BLEACHERS 
O|
N|Kit and Holly are necking underneath some bleachers. In the 
N|distance we hear the NOISE of the football practice. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|My stomach's growling.
O|
C|KIT
U|There's an old Fudgesicle over there. You want it?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|No.
O|
N|Kit laughs, and starts her laughing, too. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Somebody else is going to get it.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I don't care.
O|
C|KIT
U|Kids eat that kind of stuff in Korea.
O|
N|At this her expression turns serious. 
O|
N,G|SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N,G|Kit in CLOSEUP at a fence in the feedlot, an intense look on his 
N|face. 
O|
N,G|VARIOUS ANGLES of the cattle in their pens. They look numb and 
N|morose. 
O|
N,G|TIGHT SHOT of Holly necking with Kit. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|In the stench and slime of the feedlot, he'd remember how I 
U|looked the night before, how I ran my hand through his hair and 
U|traced the outline of his lips with my fingertip. He wanted to 
U|die with me, and I dreamed of being lost forever in his arms.
O|
S|EXT. HOLLY'S BACK YARD 
O|
N,G|TIGHT SHOT of Holly's pet catfish in a bowl. 
O|
N,G,T|CUT TO WIDE SHOT of Holly with gloves on carrying the fish 
N|through the garden in back of her house. She looks around warily, 
N|then throws it out among the melons. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|The whole time, the only thing I did wrong was throwing out my 
U|fish when he got sick. Later I got a new one, but this incident 
U|kept on bothering me and I turned to Kit.
O|
S|EXT. STREET 
O|
N|Kit and Holly walk down a sidewalk in the residential part of 
N|town. Kit is consoling her about the fish. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|I didn't mind telling Kit about stuff like this, cause strange 
U|things happened in his life, too, and some of the stuff he did 
U|was strange.
O|
S|EXT. FEEDLOT 
O|
N|Kit steps on top of a dead cow, as though to convince himself 
N|that it is dead. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|For instance,. he faked his signature whenever he used it, to 
U|keep other people from forging important papers with his name...
O|
S|INT. HOLLY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT 
O|
N,G|The CAMERA DOLLIES BACK to reveal Kit lying in bed. His attitude 
N|suggests that this is a scene of her fantasy. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|And as he lay in bed, in the middle of the night, he always heard 
U|a noise like somebody was holding a seashell against his ear. And 
U|sometimes he'd see me coming toward him in beautiful white robes, 
U|and I'd put my cold hand on his forehead.
O|
S|EXT. RIVERBANK 
O|
N|Kit puts on his jacket and heads down a riverbank to the edge of 
N|the water. Holly comes into view, buttoning her blouse. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Did it go the way it 'uz supposed to?
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Is that all there is to it?
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah.
O|
N|Her questions make him uncomfortable. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Gosh, what was everybody talking about?
O|
C|KIT
U|Don't ask me.
O|
N|Silence. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well. I'm glad it's over... For a while I was afraid I might die 
U|before it happened... Had a wreck, some deal like that.
O|
N|Kit points to an uprooted tree lying in the water a hundred feet 
N|away. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You see where that tree fell in the water?
O|
N|He climbs back up the bank. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yeah... The river must've washed the roots away... You don't care 
U|about anything I say, though.
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. NEIGHBORING AREA 
O|
N|Holly walks down the bank of the river. Kit falls in behind her, 
N,G|carrying a large stone. The CAMERA DOLLIES with them. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You know what I think?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What?
O|
C|KIT
U|That we should crunch our hands with this stone. That way we'd 
U|never forget what happened today.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|But it would hurt.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, that's the point, stupid.
O|
N|She gives him a cold look. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Don't call me stupid.
O|
C|KIT
U|Okay, but I'm going to keep it for a souvenir...
O|
N|He throws it awav and picks up a smaller one. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Or maybe one that's lighter.
O|
N|He walks over to the car. A bridge is visible in the distance. 
O|
S|EXT. FIELD 
O|
N|Kit releases a large red balloon into the evening sky. A small 
N|basket is fixed to the bottom of the balloon. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit made a solemn vow that he would always stand beside me and 
U|let nothing come between us. He wrote this out in writing, put 
U|the paper in a box with some of our little tokens and things, 
U|then sent it off in a balloon he'd found while on the garbage 
U,P|route. (pause) His heart was filled with longing as he watched it 
U|drift off. Something must've told him that we'd never live these 
U|days of happiness again, that they were gone forever.
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. CATTAILS 
O|
N|Holly's dog bounds through a stand of cattails. Holly speaks 
N|angrily to her father, who walks toward the dog with a gun. We do 
N|not hear their voices, only music. He shoots the dog and Holly 
N|runs off in horror. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Then, sure enough, Dad found out I'd been running around behind 
U|his back. He was madder than I'd ever seen him. As punishment for 
U|deceiving him, he went and shot my dog.
O|
S|EXT. BRIDGE 
O|
N|Holly's father drags a duffel bag out of his truck and dumps it 
N|over the side of a bridge, into a river. It goes under briefly, 
N|then bobs to the surface. The current takes it off. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. MUSIC SCHOOL 
O|
N|Holly sits in the second-story window of a music school. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|He made me take extra music lessons every day after school and 
U|wait there till he came to pick me up. He said that if the piano 
U|didn't keep me off the streets. maybe the clarinet would.
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. BILLBOARD 
O|
N|Kit approaches Holly's father, who is hard at work, painting a 
N|billboard along a deserted stretch of road. Kit is eating a 
N|peach. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Sure is pretty.
O|
C|FATHER
U|What'd you come out here for?
O|
C|KIT
U|I wasn't aware there was any law against it.
O|
N|Kit sees that this kind of talk will not do. He is silent for a 
N|moment, then throws the peach away. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You know Holly... well. she means a lot to me, sir.
O|
N|Holly's father goes solemnly about his business. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You know, before I met her, nobody could ask me how I was doing 
U|with my girl. Matter of fact, I didn't really have one.
O|
C|FATHER
U|Is that right?
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah.
O|
N|Kit walks off a few paces and leans against the father's truck. 
O|
C|KIT
U|It okay me leaning on your Willys here?
O|
N|Holly's father does not answer. 
O|
C|KIT
U,P|Listen. I got a lot of respect for her, sir. (pause) That's about 
U|as good a one as I know to tell you.
O|
C|FATHER
U,P|Well, it's not good enough. (pause) Just what do you think would 
U|happen to her if she stuck around with you, Kit? Guy like you.
O|
C|KIT
U|She'd get along okay. And if she didn't, well, she could take 
U|off, just take off, (and) I wouldn't mind... I'd always tell 
U|people I deserved it.
O|
N|Holly's father comes to his feet and fixes Kit in the eye. 
O|
C|FATHER
U|You get out of here. I don't want you hanging around any more. 
U|You understand?
O|
N|He lets these words sink in, then returns to his seat. 
O|
C|FATHER
U,P|(shaking his head) You're something.
O|
N|Kit waves goodbye. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Takes all kinds, sir.
O|
N|He walks to his car, looking back once. 
O|
N,G|SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N,G|LONG SHOT of father at work at billboard. Kit approaches Holly's 
N|house, collects the evening newspaper from the sidewalk, and 
N|knocks on the front door. Holly's father picks her up from a park 
N|outside the music school. Kit, when he gets no answer, enters the 
N|house. The jeep, with Holly and her father, rumbles down a 
N|residential street. 
O|
S|INT. HOLLY'S HOUSE 
O|
N|Kit is going through Holly's dresser, throwing her things into a 
N|suitcase. A pistol is visible in his back pocket. Suddenly, 
N|Holly's father appears in the dresser mirror, standing at the end 
N|of the hall outside the bedroom. 
O|
N,G|Kit walks forward to greet him. The CAMERA DOLLIES with him. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi.
O|
C|FATHER
U|What're you doing?
O|
C|KIT
U|I've got a gun here, sir. It's always a good idea to have one 
U|around.
O|
N|Kit draws the pistol. He is shaken, though he must have seen this 
N|coming. 
O|
C|FATHER
U|What do you think you're doing? Go on, get out of here.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I got it all planned... and I'm taking Holly off with me.
O|
N|He looks at Holly, who has appeared on the landing, uncertain 
N|what her reaction to all this will be. 
O|
C|FATHER
U|Okay, boy, I'm turning you over to the authorities.
O|
N|Kit steps forward as Holly's father starts down the stairs. 
O|
C|KIT
U|What for?
O|
C|FATHER
U|For coming onto my property... With a gun.
O|
C|KIT
U|No, you're not either.
O|
C,E|FATHER (o.c.)
U|Yeah? Why not?
O|
C|KIT
U|Cause I can't allow it.
O|
N|Holly's father stares at him for a moment, then continues down 
N|the stairs. Kit starts forward. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey... Hey, wait a minute.
O|
N|His shout arrests Holly's father at the bottom of the stairs. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Suppose I shot you. How'd that be? Huh?
O|
N|Holly's father does not move. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You want to hear what it sounds like?
O|
N|He fires a shot into the stairs. The report is deafening in the 
N|enclosure of the stairwell. They exchange looks. Kit's breathing 
N|is heavy. 
O|
S|INT. LIVING ROOM 
O|
N|Holly's father turns and walks into the living room. Kit rushes 
N|down the stairs. Holly shouts his name. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey, where you going?
O|
N|He fires two shots into Holly's father, who slowly sinks to the 
N|floor. Kit unlimbers his shoulder. Holly rushes down the stairs 
N|to her father's side. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Daddy... This is Holly... Are you going to be okay?
O|
N|Her father does not respond. She looks back at Kit, who avoids 
N|her gaze. She gets to her feet and walks nto the kitchen. Kit 
N|follows her. 
O|
S|INT. KITCHEN 
O|
N|Kit closes the back door to the house. 
O|
C|KIT
U|I came in the front.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|How bad off is he?
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|I can look and see.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|We better call the doctor... Listen. I'll say how it happened, 
U|part I saw.
O|
C,P|KIT (sighs)
U|Well... I don't think that'd work.
O|
N|This startles Holly. 
O|
S|INT. LIVING ROOM 
O|
N|Kit touches the father's heart. 
O|
C|KIT
U|He don't need a doctor.
O|
N|He walks back into the kitchen. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Are you sure?
O|
C|KIT
U|You don't believe me, see for yourself.
O|
N|Holly stares at her father. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. HOLLY'S HOUSE - DUSK 
O|
N,G|LOW ANGLE of Holly's house silhouetted against the evening sky. 
N|On sound the NOISE of CICADAS. 
O|
S|INT. CELLAR 
O|
N|Kit drags Holly's father into the cellar. sets him down gently, 
N|then eases out of the room, taking a discarded toaster with him. 
O|
S|INT. KITCHEN - NIGHT 
O|
N|Kit comes up the steps from the cellar. Holly stands at the 
N|drainboard. 
O|
C|KIT
U|I found a toaster.
O|
N|He sets down the toaster and walks over to Holly's side. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Listen, honey. I don't want to...
O|
N|She slaps him across the forehead. He walks into the now darkened 
N|living room, and sits down at the piano. After a moment Holly 
N|follows him. 
O|
S|INT. LIVING ROOM ~ NIGHT 
O|
N|Holly sits down on the sofa across from Kit. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Listen, maybe we ought to tell somebody about this.
O|
C|KIT
U|You said that once already... Too late now.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Why?
O|
C|KIT
U|They're not going to listen to me. You either. Are you kidding?
O|
N|A silence follows. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Suppose the neighbors heard the noise?
O|
C|KIT
U|Wouldn't be funny... Listen, I'll be back in a while.
O|
N|He gets up and heads for the front door. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Oh... You want to call the police, that's fine. Just won't be so 
U|hot for me.
O|
N|There are tears in Holly's eyes now. They exchange looks as Kit 
N|opens the front door. Then he leaves. Holly settles back on the 
N|couch. 
O|
S|EXT. HOLLY'S HOUSE - NIGHT 
O|
S|The house by night. 
O|
S|INT. HALLWAY AND BEDROOM - NIGHT 
O|
N|Holly comes up the stairs, smoking a cigarette. She walks to her 
N,G|bedroom window. The CAMERA DOLLIES with her. Outside, under the 
N|street lamp, she can see two boys trading secrets. 
O|
S|INT. TRAIN STATION - NIGHT 
O|
N|Kit puts fifty cents into a gramophone booth. He clears his voice 
N|as the machine whirs Into operation. 
O|
C|KIT
U|My girl Holly and I have decided to kill ourselves, same way I 
U|did her dad. Big decision huh? Well, the reasons are obvious, and 
U|I don't have time to go into them right now... One thing, 
U|though... He was provoking me when I popped him. That's what it 
U|was like, a POP.
O|
N,G|Inserts of the booth's mechanism
N|a light panel measuring off the seconds and the needle on the 
N|record. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Course nobody's coming out of this thing happy, especially not 
U|us. You can't deny we've had fun, though. Which is more than I 
U|can say for some.... Mmmm. that's the end of the message. I run 
U|out of things to say... Thank you.
O|
N|He gets these last words in just as his time is up. He gets the 
N|record and steps outside the booth, into the lobby of a train 
N|station. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S,G|INT. HOLLY'S LIVING ROOM - SERIES OF ANGLES - NIGHT 
O|
N|Kit slams a ten-gallon can of gasoline down on the piano bench, 
N|opens it and begins pouring it out over the interior of the 
N|house, kicking aside everything that comes in his way. 
O|
S|EXT. REAR OF HOUSE - NIGHT 
O|
N|Holly comes out the screen door at the back of the house, 
N|carrying a suitcase and a painting. Kit appears with a lamp on an 
N|extension cord. He sets it down beside a victrola he has set up 
N|on a table outdoors. He puts the needle on the record he recorded 
N|earlier. Holly watches him as she retreats to the car. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
N|Kit left a record playing over and over for the District Attorney 
U|to find. He was gambling for time.
O|
N|Kit unplugs the lamp, lights a book of matches and pitches them 
N|into a puddle of gas. The back porch of the house explodes into 
N|flame. Kit runs to the car with the lamp, caught off guard by the 
N|force of the flames. They drive off. 
O|
S,G|INT./EXT HOUSE ON FIRE - SERIES OF ANGLES - NIGHT 
O|
N|Various objects around the house burst into flames
N,G|a picture we saw on the stairs. some peacock feathers, a 
N|cantaloupe and a pomegranate left on the kitchen table, the 
N|living room, Holly's bedroom, the bed itself, a doll on the bed, 
N|an A&W Root Beer sign outside the house, a water can, flames 
N,G|swirling out the front of the house. Holly's father in CLOSEUP in 
N|the cellar, sheet music lifting off the piano. etc. At length the 
N,G|CAMERA PANS over the charred frame of Holly's bed. Outside a 
N|fireman moves with a flashlight through the embers of the house. 
O|
S|INT. SCHOOL CORRIDOR 
O|
N|Holly collects her books from a locker at her school and walks 
N|anxiously down a corridor towards the exit. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit made me get my books from school, so I wouldn't fall behind. 
U|We'd be starting a new life, he said. and we'd have to change our 
U|names. His would be James. Mine would be Priscilla. We'd hide out 
U|like spies, somewhere in the North, where people didn't ask a lot 
U|of questions.
O|
S|EXT. SCHOOL - DAWN 
O|
N|Holly breaks into a run as she comes through the front door of 
N|the high school. Kit waits for her at the car. There is nobody 
N|else in sight. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|I could of snuck out the back or hid in the boiler room, I 
U|suppose, but I sensed that my destiny now lay with Kit, for 
U|better or for worse, and that it was better to spend a week with 
U|one who loved me for what I was than years of loneliness.
O|
N|She gets into the car and they drive off. 
O|
S|INT. MERCURY - DAWN 
O|
N|Kit studies Holly for signs of her mood. 
O|
C|KIT
U|How you doing?
O|
C,P|HOLLY (neutrally)
U|I'm fine. Kind of tired. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah, me too.
O|
N|The car sails down a quiet street. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. RIVER 
O|
N|The CAMERA DOLLIES with a tree floating down a swollen river. 
N|Cottonwood trees cover the far bank. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|We hid out In the wilderness,down by a river in a grove of 
U|cottonwoods. It being the flood season we built our house in the 
U|trees, with tamarisk walls and willows laid side by side to make 
U|a floor. There wasn't a plant in the forest that didn't come in 
U|handy.
O|
N,G|CLOSEUPS OF PLAIQTS - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|Details of the cottonwoods, willow and tamarisk. Music continues 
N|under. 
O|
S,G|EXT. TREEHOUSE - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|Details of Kit and Holly building the treehouse. Kit pounds a log 
N|into place wi h a tomahawk; Holly scrapes the bark off a log with 
N|a piece of flint; Kit weaves tamarisk branches through the frame 
N,G|of the house. This MONTAGE ends with a PAN DOWN the four stories 
N|of the completed treehouse. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|We planned a huge network of tunnels under the forest floor, and 
U|our first order of business every morning was to decide on a new 
U|password for the day. ow and then weld sneak out at night and 
U|steal a chicken or a bunch of corn or some melons from a melon 
U|patch... Mostly. though, we just lay on our backs and stared at 
U|the clouds and sometimes it was like being in a big marble hall. 
U|the way we talked in low voices and heard the tiniest sound.
O|
S|INT. TREEHOUSE 
O|
N,G|The CAMERA PANS off Holly's painting, a Maxwell Parrish, onto Kit 
N|asleep with his pistol beside him. then onto Holly under a quilt 
N|with a box of shells beside her. Her hand quivers once. 
O|
S|EXT. TREE TOPS 
O|
N|Kit lifts an egg out of a bird's nest at the top of a tree. He 
N|drops it down to Holly, fifty feet below. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|They hadn't found but one set of bones in the ashes of the house. 
U|so we knew they'd be looking for us. Kit made sure we'd be 
U|prepared.
O|
S,G|EXT. TRAIL - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|Kit cuts a piece of twine that lies across a trail, whereupon a 
N|ball full of spikes comes swinging down out of the trees. with 
N,G|lethal force. Kit ducks out of the way. We next see Kit with a 
N|dew ag around his forehead, running laps through the woods as 
N|part of his training. He exhorts himself to greater effort. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|He gave me lectures on how a gun works, how to take it apart and 
U|put it back together again. in case 1 had to carry on without 
U|him. He said that if the Devil came at me, I could shoot him with 
U|a gun.
O|
S|EXT. TAMARISK 
O|
N|Holly struggles through the undergrowth witha yoke over her 
N|shoulders, carrying wood back to the teahouse. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|One day, I carried thirty pounds of wood a distance of five 
U|miles. Another day, while hiding in the forest, I covered my eyes 
U|with makeup, to see how they'd come out.
O|
N,G|TIGHT ON HOLLY'S EYES 
O|
N|Holly has made her eyes up with mascara. 
O|
S|EXT. FRONT OF TREEHOUSE 
O|
N|Kit and Holly dance the stroll to the sound of Mickey and Sylvia 
N|on their Zenith portable. Holly shows a better sense of rhythm 
N|than Kit. 
O|
S|EXT. MUD FLAT BESIDE RIVER - DUSK 
O|
N|Kit lifts a fish out of the river with a crude device like a 
N|lacrosse net. Holly sits on the bank In the b.g. The fish 
N|wriggles out of the net and falls back In the water. Kit 
N|struggles in vain to retrieve it. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|We had our bad moments, like any couple. Kit accused me of only 
U|being along for the ride,while at times I wished he'd fall in the 
U|river and drown, so I could watch.
O|
N|Kit throws his net down in disgust, partly at Holly. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Take a break. Red... Life of Riley, huh?
O|
N|Holly ignores him as he walks off down the flat. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Mostly. though. we got along fine and stayed in love.
O|
S|EXT. FRONT OF TREEHOUSE 
O|
N|Holly, in curlers, walks over to where Kit is shaving. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|One of the chickens died last night.
O|
N|She pats a little water on her face, dries it with a burlap 
N|towel, picks up a hatchet and walks off into the forest to cut 
N|the morning's firewood. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I almost stepped on him.
O|
S|EXT. UPPER DECK 
O|
N|Holly sits on the upper deck of the treehouse, reading Kon-Tiki 
N|aloud to Kit. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|"The Kon-Tiki in motion was a little different from what it 
U|usually was in such conditions. We had become sensitive to 
U|changes in the rhythm of the logs. I thought at once of suction 
U|from the coast, which was drawing near, and was continually out 
U|on the deck and up the mast..."
O|
C|KIT
N|He was nervous.
O|
N|Holly looks at him for a moment, trying to understand the meaning 
N|of this interruption, then resumes
O|
O|
C|HOLLY
U|"Time passed. At dawn, just before six, Torstein came hurrying 
U|down from the masthead. He could see a whole line of small palm-
U|clad islands far ahead..."
O|
S|EXT. RIVER 
O|
N|Holly walks down the edge of the river, fresh from her bath. 
N|wrapped in a white sheet. The river is raging, with white caps 
N|visible in places. 
O|
S|EXT. FOREST 
O|
N|A strange animal, not a deer or a horse, but like them, moves 
N|languidly through the thick of the forest. Holly puts down her 
N|binoculars, amazed. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|I grew to love the forest. The cooing of the doves and the hum of 
U|dragonflies in the air made it always seem lonesome and like 
U|everybody's dead and gone...
O|
S|EXT. CROW'S NEST 
O|
N|Kit sits in the crow's nest at the top of the treehouse, browsing 
N|through a copy of National Geographic. amused by what he sees, 
N|looking around to find somebody he can share it with. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|When the leaves rustled overhead, it was like the spirits were 
U|whispering about all the little things that bothered 'em.
O|
N,G|TIGHT ON SLIDES 
O|
N|An array of stereo slides is spread out on the leaves of the 
N|forest floor. Holly puts one in her stereopticon and studies it. 
N|Kit greets her as he walks by with a log over his shoulder. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|One day, while taking a look at some vistas in Dad's 
U|stereopticon, it hit me that I was just this little girl, born in 
U|Texas, whose father was a sign painter and who had only just so 
U|many years to live ...
O|
N,G|TIGHT ON SLIDES 
O|
N,G|With each of her thoughts a slide appears
N|a canal in Brazil, a camel boy in front of the Great Pyramid. 
N|some cows standing in a fjord with a steamship in the distance, a 
N|mother with her child, a woman playing the piano as another woman 
N|looks on, a family on a lawn, a soldier in a wheatfield 
N|whispering something into his girlfriend's ear. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|... It sent a chill down my spine, and I thought
U|Where would I be this very moment if Kit had never met me? ... Or 
U|killed anybody? This very moment... If my Mom had never met my 
U|Dad? If she'd of never died?... And what's the man I'll marry 
U|going to look like? What's he doing right this minute? ... Is he 
U|thinking about me now, by some coincidence, even though he 
U|doesn't know me? Does it show on his face?
O|
S|EXT. RIVER 
O|
N|Kit is fishing with his crude net. He stops briefly to watch a 
N|truck passing down the highway In the distance, then goes back to 
N|work. This stretch of river seems dangerously close to 
N|civilization. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|For days afterward I lived in dread. At times I wished I could 
U|fall asleep and be taken off to some magical land, but this never 
U|happened.
O|
N|Kit, frustrated draws his pistol from his back pocket and looks 
N|around for the fish that has been eluding him. He does not see, 
N,G|as we do, a MAN watching him from across the river. He fires at 
N|the fish. misses it and fires again. The Man across the river 
N|hesitates. then moves back into the undergrowth. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S,G|SERIES OF ANGLES - TREEHOUSE 
O|
N|Holly is off somewhere in the forest. putting on lipstick. Kit 
N|sits on watch in the crowls nest, a pair of binoculars close at 
N|hand. On sound, over the music, we hear voices whispering to 
N|them. Kit sees a movement in the brush. A moment later a figure 
N|appears, a hunter, carrying a gun. Kit shouts a code word to 
N|Holly. She takes off running, as Kit clambers down the planks of 
N|the treehouse and collects his shotgun. He tells her to hide, 
N|runs through the woods and dives in a hole, under a cover of 
N,G|leaves. She jumps behind a log. Several shots of the still forest 
N|follow, then the hunters appear, a shoulder here. pant leg there. 
N|The music ends as one of them cocks his gun. They pass right over 
N|the hole where Kit is hiding. Kit rises up slowly, gives them a 
N|gentlemanly word of warning, then shoots all three in the back. 
N|He checks to see if anybody else is with them, then calls weakly 
N|out to Holly. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Holly!
O|
N|Holly picks her way through the brush, back to the treehouse. 
O|
S|EXT. TREEHOUSE 
O|
N|They are packed up and ready to leave. Kit slams the trunk of the 
N|Mercury. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Get in the car, Red.
O|
N|He takes a bird cage off the roof of the car and throws it away. 
O|
C|KIT
U|We don't need this.
O|
N|He climbs a ladder, looks In the cabin of the treehouse to see if 
N|they've left anything behind, then jumps down. He flips over the 
N|wicker hemisphere that covers their rooster. The rooster walks 
N|off, free. He surveys the woods, then looks back at Holly, who 
N|returns his look and gets in the car. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit felt bad about shooting those men in the backs. 
U|but he said they'd come in like that. and they would've played it 
U|as down and dirty as they could, and besides, held overheard them 
U|whispering about how they were only interested in the reward 
U|money. With lawmen it wouldtve been different. They were out 
U|there to get a job done and they deserved a fair chance. But not 
U|a bounty hunter.
O|
S,G|EXT. BADLANDS - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N,G|We see a flat expanse of the Badlands. The Mercury appears over 
N|the horizon and heads toward a solitary house. A man appears at 
N|the door of the house, Kit's friend, Cato. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|We went to hide out with a friend of Kit's from the days of the 
U|garbage route.
O|
S|EXT. CATO'S HOUSE. 
O|
N|Kit gets out of the car, takes a chicken from the back seat and 
N|walks over to face Cato. He keeps a safe distance. 
O|
C|KIT
U|How you doing, Cato?
O|
C|CATO
U|Not bad.
O|
N|Kit steps forward, rifle in hand, and offers Cato the chicken. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Take that sombitch.
O|
N|Cato takes it, a wan smile on his face. 
O|
C|KIT
U|What you been doing?
O|
C|CATO
U|Running this place for a fella in town. Nothing much to speak of.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I don't notice us hustling trash, either.
O|
N|Kit walks over to inspect the interior of Cato's house. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Where'd you get them antlers?
O|
C,E|CATO (o.c.)
U|They come with the house.
O|
N|He refers to a set of deer antlers, mounted over the door. Kit 
N|looks inside, then back at Cato. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Oh, Cato, I want you to meet my girl, Holly Sargis.
O|
N|Holly smiles and says hello. 
O|
S|EXT. PICNIC TABLE 
O|
N|They sit at a picnic table under a tree, having lunch. Holly is 
N|telling a joke at Kit's urging. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Did you hear the one about the guy in the nuthouse that went 
U|around naked, with only a hat and gloves on, and this nurse came 
U|up to him and said, "Hey, what... You can't walk around that 
U|way," and the guy says, "What's the matter? Nobody comes around 
U|here." Anyway, the nurse says, "What do you have the hat and 
U|gloves on for, then?" And the guy says, "Well, you never know."
O|
N|Cato bursts into laughter. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Isn't that funny?
O|
N|Cato stops laughing. He wants to give Kit the right answer. 
O|
C|CATO
U|Yeah, I guess.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|She plays the clarinet, too.
O|
N|Holly smiles politely. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Don't you ever get bored around here?
O|
C|CATO
U|Sometimes. The other day, though, an old boy was plowing in the 
U|field over there, found some old Spanish coins.
O|
C|KIT
P|(suspicious)
U|That's what he told you, huh?
O|
C,E|CATO (o.c.)
U|No, he showed me one.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What'd they look like?
O|
C,E|CATO (o.c.)
U|Kind of round, like so... Gold. I'll show you if you want.
O|
N|Kit and Holly eye one another. Kit sees the chance to make a 
N|fortune here. He is careful to conceal his feelings, though. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Let me finish my supper.
O|
N|Holly, big-eyed, bites into an apple. 
O|
S|EXT. FIELD 
O|
N|Kit, Holly and Cato walk out to the field where Cato heard the 
N|coins were found. Holly picks up a pot lid. 
O|
C|KIT
U|We're thinking of going down to Texas. Holly wants to visit some 
U|of her people down there, but after that I don't know... They say 
U|Mexico's nice.
O|
N|Cato stops. 
O|
C|CATO
U|Kit... Maybe I'd better get a shovel.
O|
C|KIT
U|Okay.
O|
C|CATO
U|I'll catch up with you.
O|
C|KIT
U|Okay.
O|
N|Kit walks out into the field to join Holly. Cato turns back. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I found a lid. It was laying on the ground over there.
O|
C|KIT
U|Put that down. It's dirty.
O|
N|Holly tosses the lid at him. He picks up some dirt clods and 
N|starts throwing them at her. She squeals and dodges. CATO watches 
N|them for a while, indecisive, then turns and runs. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (o.c.)
U|Hey, you're throwing big ones!
O|
N|Suddenly, Kit spots Cato. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Cato!
O|
N|Cato jumps over a trough and comes to a stop in front of his 
N|truck, a water tanker. 
O|
C|CATO
U|The shovel's in the truck.
O|
N|Kit raises his gun and shoots Cato through the belly. CATO falls, 
N|then gets to his feet and stumbles toward the front door of the 
N|house. Kit follows him for a while, then skips forward and opens 
N|the door for him. 
O|
S|INT. BEDROOM 
O|
N|Cato falls on his bed and rolls over to face Kit. Kit walks in 
N|calmly and picks up a photo on the bureau. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit never let on why he'd shot Cato. He said that just talking 
U|about it could bring us bad luck and that right now we needed all 
U|the luck we could get.
O|
N|Kit sets the photo down. 
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|You never told me about her.
O|
N|He looks under the bed, then walks out the door. 
O|
S|INT. LIVING ROOM 
O|
N|Holly stands near a window. smoking a cigarette. Kit sets his 
N|rifle down and surveys the room, which is cluttered with odds and 
N|ends. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Look at all this junk.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|How's he doing?
O|
C|KIT
P|(shrugging)
U|I got him in the stomach.
O|
N|Holly sits down. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Is he upset?
O|
C|KIT
U|He didn't say anything to me about it.
O|
N|He takes a drag off her cigarette and gives it back to her. He 
N|leafs idly through a catalogue on the table, then walks over to 
N|look in the bedroom. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Bunch of junk.
O|
N|Inside the bedroom Cato is still conscious, but his breathing is 
N|heavy. Kit walks back to Holly's side. HOLLY is looking through 
N|the catalogue. 
O|
C|KIT
U|He stole that cage. I saw him doing it.
O|
N|Kit wants this to have been an execution, not a murder. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Whatcha looking in there for? We can't afford any of that.
O|
C,P|HOLLY (defensive)
U|Just looking.
O|
S|INT. BEDROOM 
O|
N|As they bicker in the other room, Cato inspects his face in a 
N|mirror. 
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|Why don't you go in there and keep him company for a while?
O|
N|Holly walks in the door. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Hi.
O|
N|She sits down. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|That your spider in there? in that bottle?
O|
C|CATO
U|Yeah.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What do you feed him?
O|
C|CATO
U|Oh, flies... grasshoppers when I can catch 'em.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Does he bite?
O|
C|CATO
U|He never bit me.
O|
N|We hear the sound of a car HORN in the distance. HOLLY
N|turns and looks. 
O|
S|EXT. CATO'S HOUSE 
O|
N|A 1951 Studebaker approaches the house. There are two people 
N|inside, a BOY and a GIRL. Kit comes out the front door. The Boy 
N|and Girl get out, but seeing Kit, they wait by the car. 
O|
C|BOY
U|Hi. where's Cato?
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, he's gone.
O|
C|BOY
U|Gone? ... Where?
O|
C|KIT
U|He said not to tell.
O|
C,E|BOY (o.c.)
U|Oh yeah?
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah, he said for you to give us a lift into town. You're the 
U|ones with the Studebaker, aren't you?
O|
N|The Boy hesitates a second, then starts for the house. 
O|
C|BOY
U|Well, I just need to pick up something in the house.
O|
N|Kit draws his gun. 
O|
C|KIT
U|No... You go in there, I'll have to kill you.
O|
C|BOY
U|What's going on?
O|
C|KIT
U|Can't afford to take chances.
O|
N|The Girl is baffled. 
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|Come on out of there, young lady.
O|
N|Kit waves them out into the neighboring field with him. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Let's us step out in this field here.
O|
N|The Boy raises his hands. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Nah, skip that... I'm going to have to keep an eye on you, 
U|though.
O|
C|BOY
U|Okay.
O|
C|KIT
U|You don't mind?
O|
N|Holly steps out the front door and smiles at the girl. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Hi.
O|
S|EXT. FIELD 
O|
N|Holly and the girl walk through the field toward a storm cellar 
N|where the boys are waiting for them. The wind is blowing hard. 
O|
C|GIRL
U|What's going to happen to Jack and me?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|You have to ask Kit. He says frog, I jump.
O|
C|GIRL
U|Okay.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What's your friend's name?
O|
C|GIRL
U|Jack.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|You love him?
O|
C|GIRL
U|I don't know.
O|
N|Holly thinks about this for a moment. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I've got to stick by Kit... He feels trapped.
O|
C|GIRL
U|Yeah. I can imagine.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, I've felt that way, hadn't you?
O|
S|EXT. STORM CELLAR 
O|
N|Kit guides the couple into the storm cellar. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Okay, you two, down in the cellar.
O|
N|They head down the steps into the cellar, the Girl first. Kit 
N|stops the Boy for a moment. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You promise to stay down there for an hour?
O|
C|BOY
U|Yeah.
O|
C|KIT
U|You expect me to believe that?
O|
N|He looks back at Holly, as though to ask whether he should. 
O|
C|BOY
U|Yeah.
O|
N|Kit nods and waves the Boy down into the cellar. The Boy 
N|disappears. Kit shuts the door. 
O|
C|KIT
U|I'm going to shut the door.
O|
N|Kit sticks a pin through the latch, hesitates, then fires two 
N|shots down through a seam into the cellar. He retreats to Holly's 
N|side. a little surprised at what he did. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Think I got 'em?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I don't know.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I'm not going down there and look.
O|
N|He starts running away from the cellar. Holly follows him. A 
N|storm is gathering on the horizon. Kit offers Holly his hand as 
N|they run. but Holly does not see this. 
O|
S|INT. CATO'S BEDROOM 
O|
N|Cato lies motionless, on his bed. Holly steps forward and touches 
N|him with a yardstick, like a child touching a snake. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Suddenly, I was thrown into a state of shock...
O|
S|EXT. HOUSE AND BOX CAR 
O|
N|Kit, rifle in hand, lumbers out a side door of the house with 
N|Cato over his shoulder. He steers for a nearby box car that 
N|evidently served Cato as a shed. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit was the most trigger happy person I'd ever met. He claimed 
U|that as long as you're playing for keeps and the law is coming at 
U|you, it's considered okay to shoot all witnesses. You had to take 
U|the consequences, though, and not whine about it later.
O|
N|Kit opens the doors of the box car. and dumps Cato inside. Before 
N|closing the doors he checks Cato's shoes. to see if they are the 
N|ones he gave him. Holly looks on from the side of the house, 
N|twenty yards away. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|We're in for it now... If they catch us.
O|
N|Kit looks cornered and defensive. Holly thinks for a moment. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What'd you put him in there for?
O|
C,P|KIT (shrugging)
U|Just to keep him out of the sun.
O|
N|Kit heads out of sight around the rear of the box car. Holly goes 
N|back inside. Kit walks up and down the length of the box car, 
N|talking under his breath and throwing his rifle from hand to 
N|hand, in a state. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. COUNTRY ROAD 
O|
N|Kit and Holly travel down a country road in the Studebaker. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|At this moment I didn't feel shame or fear, but just kind of 
U|blah, like when you're sitting there and all the water's run out 
N|of the bathtub.
O|
S|INT. CAR 
O|
N|Kit, driving, turns to Holly. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You tired?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yeah.
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah, you look tired... Listen, honey. when all this is over, I'm 
U|going to sit down and buy you a big, thick steak.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I don't want a steak.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, we'll see about that... Hey, lookie.
O|
N|He nods at a wrecked car lying in a ditch beside the road. 
O|
C|KIT
U|They're probably going to blame that on me, too, bastards.
O|
S,G|VIGILANTE MONTAGE - SERIES OF ANGLES - BLACK AND WHITE 
O|
N,G|Various shots characterizing the Midwest's reaction to Kit and 
N|Holly
N|a deserted street, law officers posing with their guns and 
N|ammunition, children walking from class under armed guard, a 
N|detective in a trench coat pointing at a pump, a woman calling in 
N|her children at dusk, a county official posing with the county 
N|records, the court reporter with a book of signatures, vigilantes 
N|signing up and being deputized, then riding through the streets 
N|of their town in the backs of pickups, a priest among them, etc. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|The whole country was out looking for us, for who knew where Kit 
U|would strike next? Sidewalks were deserted. Stores closed their 
U|doors and drew their blinds. Posses and vigilante committees were 
U|set up from Texas to North Dakota. Children rode back and forth 
U|to school under heavy guard. A famous detective was brought in 
U|from Boston. He could find no clues ... My clarinet teacher said 
U|I probably wasn't responsible, but others said I was. Then, on 
U|Thursday, the Governor of Oklahoma sent out the National Guard to 
U|stand watch at the Federal Reserve Bank in Tulsa when word got 
U|out that Kit meant to rob it... It was like the Russians had 
U|invaded.
O|
S|EXT. RICH MAN'S HOUSE 
O|
N|Kit and Holly approach a large Victorian mansion. Kit rings the 
N|bell. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|We needed supplies, so we went to a rich man's house. Kit figured 
U|it'd be safer and quicker than shopping in the downtown... A maid 
U|came to the door.
O|
N|A MAID opens the door. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi, I come to check the meter. My tools are in here.
O|
N|He nods at the suitcase he is carrying. The Maid looks at Holly. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Oh, this Is Holly. She's from Texas.
O|
N|Kit winks at Holly. The Maid brushes her ears to indicate that 
N|she is deaf, then stands back to let them pass. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Later we found out she was deaf and we hadn't even known it.
O|
C|KIT
U|Excuse me.
O|
N|They enter the house. 
O|
S|INT. LIVING ROOM 
O|
N|Kit leads the others into the living room. The owner of the 
N|house, a RICH MAN, sits in a chair in the corner, reviewing some 
N|prints. At the sight of Kit he comes to his feet. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi.
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Yes?
O|
C|KIT
U|This your place?
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Yes.
O|
N|Kit looks around, overwhelmed by the luxury of the mansion. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Sorry to barge in on you. Anybody else here besides you two?
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|No.
O|
N|The Rich Man is wary. Kit nearly forgets to explain himself. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Good deal... Oh, uh, we're on the run and we'd like to hang out 
U|here for a while. Couple of hours, maybe. How'd that be?
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Stay as long as you like.
O|
N,G|TIGHT ON MURAL 
O|
N,G|The CAMERA PANS across a mural of some pastoral scene. 
O|
S,G|INT. HOUSE - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|Holly runs her finger around the lip of a crystal glass. It makes 
N|a soft chiming noise. Kit, sitting at the end of a table opposite 
N|her, picks up a dinner bell, rings it, then puts It down and 
N|regards her solemnly. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Next time I ring that, it means time to clear out.
O|
N,G|Holly, in the next angle, has moved a bust off its pedestal onto 
N|the corner of a piano. She thinks better of this and restores the 
N|bust to its original position. 
O|
N|Kit sniffs at a glass of sherry, then takes a picture off the 
N|mantel and studies it. 
O|
N|Holly, at the other end of the room. tests the comforts of a 
N|chair. This done. she moves to another chair and tests it, too. 
N|She makes her hand limp. She watches it swing back and forth. 
O|
N|Kit sits in the chair which the Rich Man occupied earlier. He has 
N|a thoughtful look on his face. There is a dictaphone on the table 
N|beside him. 
O|
S|INT. HALLWAY 
O|
N|Holly takes a cane out of the umbrella rack, looks around 
N|furtively, then steps out a side door of the house. 
O|
S|EXT. LAWN 
O|
N|Holly walks down the slope of the lawn. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|I left Kit in the parlor and went for a stroll outside the house. 
U|The day was quiet and serene, but I didn't notice, for I was deep 
U|in thought, and not even thinking about how to slip off.
O|
N|Across the way she can see a long driveway, with a border of lush 
N|greenery and a sleepy cat standing in the sun. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|The world was like a faraway planet to which I could never 
U|return... I thought what a fine place it was, full of things for 
U|people to look into and enjoy.
O|
S,G|INT. LIVING ROOM - TIGHT ON KIT 
O|
N|Kit talks into the dictaphone, recording some advice for young 
N|people. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Listen to your parents and teachers. They got a line on most 
U|things, so don't treat them like enemies. There's always a chance 
U,P|you could learn something. (he pauses for a moment) Try to keep 
U|an open mind. Try to understand the viewpoint of others. Consider 
U|the minority opinion, but try to get along with the majority 
U,P|opinion once it's accepted. (again he pauses) Course Holly and 
U|I've had fun, even if it has been rushed, and... so far we're 
U,P|doing fine. hadn't got caught. (annoyed) Excuse the grammar.
O|
N|He sets down the dictaphone. puts out his cigarette and leans 
N|back in his chair, lost in reflection. 
O|
S|INT. COVERED ROOM 
O|
N|Holly enters the room where the Rich Man and his Maid have been 
N|put. The furniture and paintings are covered with sheets, 
N|suggesting that for some purpose, known only to the family, the 
N|room has been set aside. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Hi... Kit's downstairs playing with the dictaphone... Sometimes 
U|he acts like there's something wrong with his bean.
O|
N|She leans against the window. behind the couch where the Rich Man 
N|is sitting. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Hope nothing ever goes wrong with mine.
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Think he's crazy, huh?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I don't know... He's kind of odd.
O|
N|She sits down beside the Rich Man. 
O|
C,P|HOLLY (shyly)
U|They claim I've got him wrapped around my little finger, but I 
U|never told him to shoot anybody.
O|
N|She looks the room over. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Gosh, I like your house.
O|
C|RICH MAN
O|Thank you.
O|
N|We hear the NOISE of a doorbell. 
O|
S|INT. LIVING ROOM 
O|
N|Kit puts down a copy of the Wall Street Journal and locates his 
N|pistol. He starts for the door, then remembers that he moved a 
N|picture from the mantel. He puts the picture back in place, 
N|checks the chamber of his pistol and leaves the room. 
O|
S|EXT. FRONT OF HOUSE 
O|
N|Kit opens the front door to find a VISITOR on the stoop. The 
N|Visitor carries a roll of blueprints under his arm. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi.
O|
C|VISITOR
U|Hi... ah, Mister Scarborough here?
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah, but the thing about him, he's down with the flu. He's sick.
O|
C|VISITOR
U|Really?
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah. I'd invite you inside, except it's contagious. Don't want 
U|to start an epidemic.
O|
C|VISITOR
U|No, of course not. It's only that he called last night and asked 
U|if I could come by.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, he didn't have it last night.
O|
N|The Visitor is puzzled by Kit's presence in the house, and even 
N|more by his behavior. He takes out a note pad. 
O|
C|KIT
U|What's that?
O|
C|VISITOR
U|Well, I'd like to leave a message, if that's okay.
O|
C|KIT
U|Sure.
O|
N|The Visitor jots something down and hands the note to Kit. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You have to excuse me now. I have to go back inside. Bye.
O|
N|Kit closes the door. 
O|
S|INT. STAIRWELL 
O|
N|Kit hides the Visitor's note in a vase and bounds up the stairs 
N|to the first landing. 
O|
C|KIT
U|We're out of here, Tex.
O|
N|He turns back down the stairs. On a rack at the bottom he finds a 
N|hat and coat. He takes them with him. 
O|
S|INT. COVERED ROOM 
O|
N|Kit walks into the room where he left the Rich Man and his maid. 
N|He now carries a bag of groceries under his arm, 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi, whatcha doing?
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Just thinking.
O|
C|KIT
U|Good a way to kill time as any... She okay?
O|
N|He points to the Maid with his pistol. 
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Yes.
O|
C|KIT
U|Listen, ah ... We're going to take the Cadillac for a while. 
U|How'd that be?
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Fine.
O|
C|KIT
U|Don't worry, I won't let her drive.
O|
N|He means Holly. 
O|
C,E|KIT (continuing)
U|Oh, and here's a list of everything we borrowed. Car's on there, 
U|too.
O|
N|He gives the list to the Rich Man and signals the Maid to 
N|accompany them out. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Okay, ma'am, let's go . . . come on.
O|
S|INT. HALLWAY 
O|
N|Kit points the Rich Man and his Maid into a closet. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You're my friend, aren't you?
O|
C|RICH MAN
U|Yes.
O|
C|KIT
U|Okay, no monkey business then.
O|
N|He locks the door of the closet once they are inside, starts off, 
N|then remembers something, takes a handkerchief out of his pocket 
N|and wipes his fingerprints off the door handle. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Groceries.
O|
N|With this reminder he collects his bag of groceries and heads 
N|down the hall. 
O|
S|EXT. FRONT PORCH 
O|
N|Kit and Holly come out the front door. Holly wears a shawl over 
N|her head as a disguise, Kit wears the outfit he took off the Rich 
N|Man's coat rack. He carries his groceries, rifle and a silver 
N|trophy he stole from somewhere in the house. 
O|
C|KIT
U|That guy could sell that list I gave him as a sample of my 
U|handwriting.
O|
N|Holly starts off, 
O|
C,E|KIT (continuing)
U|Hey, why're you always walking ahead of me?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, why you always walking behind me?
O|
N|Kit grabs the seat of her pants. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Don't.
O|
C|KIT
U|Anybody ever done that to you before?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|No.
O|
C|KIT
U|Positive?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yes.
O|
C|KIT
U|Guess there's no way I'll ever know. For sure.
O|
N|They round a corner into the garden. 
O|
S|EXT. BACKYARD 
O|
N|They walk down the lawn toward the garage. We the TICKING OF 
N,G|SPRINKLERS off camera. 
O|
C|KIT
U|That guy must pay through the nose to keep this place up... Lawn, 
U|the gas and electric... Bagworms. Plus the upkeep on the cars... 
U|Yeah, you get a little money in your pocket, you think all your 
U|problems are solved. Well, let me tell you, they're not.
O|
N|They arrive at the Cadillac. Holly sets down her suitcase and 
N|waits for Kit to open the door for her. 
O|
N|Kit, who has forgotten this propriety, hurries back and opens the 
N|door. He throws the suitcase in the back, goes around the front 
N|of the car and gets in. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I'd like to get out of here.
O|
C|KIT
U|Soon as I start the car... and fix my hat.
O|
N|He adjusts his hat, starts the car and they drive off, past a 
N|Rolls Royce Kit has declined in favor of the Cadillac. 
O|
S,G|EXT. BADLANDS - EXTREME LONG SHOT 
O|
N|The car races across the open prairie. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Fearing there'd be roadblocks on the highways, we took off across 
U|that area known as the Great Plains. Kit told me to enjoy the 
U|scenery, and I did.
O|
S|INT. CADILLAC 
O|
N|Holly reads to Kit from a movie fan magazine. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|"Rumor
U|Pat Boone is seriously considering giving up his career so he can 
U|return to school full-time and complete his education. Fact
U|Pat has told intimates that so long as things are going well for 
U|his career, it's the education that will have to take the back 
U|seat."
O|
C|KIT
U|I don't blame him.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|"Rumor
U|Frank Sinatra and Rita Hayworth are in love... Fact
U|True, but not with each other."
O|
N|Kit chuckles at this, which pleases Holly. 
O|
S|EXT. TELEPHONE POLES 
O|
N|The Cadillac heads overland, along a line of telephone poles that 
N|stretches out clear to the horizon. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Through desert and mesa, across the endless miles of open range, 
U|we made our headlong way, steering by the telephone lines toward 
U|the mountains of Montana.
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. MOUNTAIN 
O|
N|Heat waves rise off the land. A mountain is barely discernible in 
N|the distance. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. BADLANDS 
O|
N|The Cadillac races through a herd of cows, 
O|
C,E,U|HOLLY (v.o.) Kit'd sometimes ram a cow, to save on ammo, and we'd 
U|cook it.
O|
N,G|CLOSE ON SALT GRASS 
O|
N,G|Tight shot of a clump of salt grass. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Once we had to eat a bunch of salt grass. It tasted like cabbage.
O|
S|EXT. BADLANDS - DAWN 
O|
N,G|THE CAMERA RISES over the Cadillac as it races down a deserted 
N|country road at dawn, 
O|
S|EXT. PIPELINE WAYSTATION 
O|
N|The Cadillac is stopped beside a pipeline waystation. Kit has 
N|tapped some gas off the head. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|For gas we used the leakage from the valves of the pipelines we 
U|found along the way. Drip gas is what they call it in that part 
U|of the country.
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. BADLANDS 
O|
N|The Cadillac roars through a stand of mesquite and disappears 
N|into the distance. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Little by little we approached the border. Kit was glad to leave 
U|South Dakota behind and cursed its name. He said that if the 
U|Communists ever dropped the atomic bomb, he wished they'd put it 
U|right in the middle of Rapid City.
O|
S|EXT. CAMPSITE 
O|
N|They are camped out in the middle of the badlands. Kit cooks some 
N|meat over a fire, Holly, with a map under her arm, points into 
N|the distance. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|That's Montana over there.
O|
C|KIT
U|I never been to Montana... Acquaintance of mine has, but I 
U|hadn't... Never had any reason to.
O|
N|Holly sits down and looks at the map. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|State bird's a meadowlark.
O|
S,G|EXT. PAN ACROSS BADLANDS 
O|
N,G|The CAMERA PANS across the badlands. There Is not a tree or a 
N|bush in sight. It comes to rest on Kit, who carries his rifle 
N|over his shoulders the way James Dean did in "Giant". 
O|
S,G|KIT'S P.O.V. - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N,G|Various sights of the prairie
N|a distant mountain, a wild turkey, a lizard, a burst of 
N|lightening in a cloud on the horizon, a falcon. Kit takes all 
N|this in, then he turns back to the campsite. The sun is down, 
N|just barely, and the moon has risen behind him. Holly is heard 
N|over this last shot. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|We lived in utter loneliness, neither here nor there. Kit said 
U|that solitude was a better word, cause it meant more exactly what 
U|I wanted to say. Whatever the expression, I told him we couldn't 
U|go on living this way.
O|
S|INT. CADILLAC 
O|
N|Kit and Holly sit in the back seat of the Cadillac. They have 
N|been necking. Her hair is in curlers. 
O|
C,P|KIT (responding to v.o.)
U|Why not? I mean, I'm having fun... At least I'm not bitching.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Well, I feel kind of like an animal living out here. I mean, 
U|there's no place to bathe and... no place to get anything good to 
U|eat.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I'll catch you a big trout. Soon as we get to the 
U|mountains.
O|
N|He kisses her. She gives in reluctantly. After a while they 
N|break. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Everybody loves trout.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I'm serious.
O|
N|They neck some more. then break again. Kit looks out one window, 
N|Holly out the other. 
O|
S|EXT. DISTANT TRAIN 
O|
N|A train moves slowly across the horizon. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|In the distance I saw a train making its way silently across the 
U|plain, like the caravan in "The Adventures of Marco Polo." It was 
U|our first taste of civilization in days, and I asked Kit if we 
U|could have a closer look.
O|
S|EXT. KIT - WITH FOOTBALL 
O|
N|Kit sets a football down on the ground, backs off a few steps, 
N|squats and shoots a hole through it with his pistol. To his 
N|surprise. it does not deflate. He moves closer and pounds it flat 
N|with the barrel of the pistol. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Before we left he shot a football that he considered excess 
U|baggage.
O|
N,G|TIGHT ON SUITCASE 
O|
N|Kit is burying some things from Holly's suitcase in a bucket
N|the trophy he took from the mansion, her stereopticon slides, a 
N|doll, a spark plug, a pack of Camels, etc. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Afterwards he took and buried some of our things in a bucket. He 
U|said that nobody else would know where we'd put them, and that 
U|we'd come back someday, maybe, and they'd still be sitting here, 
U|just the same, but we'd be different. And if we never got back, 
U|well, somebody might dig them up a thousand years from now and 
U|wouldn't they wonder!
O|
S|EXT. BADLANDS 
O|
N|The Cadillac speeds across the plains. A plume of dust stretches 
N|out behind it. 
O|
S|EXT. RAIL TRESTLE 
O|
N|Kit and Holly have parked the Cadillac under a rail trestle. A 
N|train is approaching. They hide beside the car. The train, a 
N|streamliner, roars overhead. When it has passed, Holly rushes up 
N|the grade to watch it disappear into the distance. Kit watches 
N|from the opposite end of the trestle. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Maybe we should've tried to hop it.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|It was going too fast.
O|
C|KIT
U|I could've pulled the car up on the tracks, slowed it down some.
O|
C,E|HOLLY (o.c.)
U|Yeah, then we'd be stuck here.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, maybe we oughta be stuck here. I'm not saying that I know.
O|
N|He stoops down and picks up a Pepsi bottle. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|You're crazy.
O|
N|He climbs over the edge of the trestle, looks back at her oddly, 
N|then leaps down to the ground. Holly walks down to meet him. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit took the bottle and spun it around, leaving to fate which 
U|direction we should take.
O|
N,G|TIGHT ON BOTTLE 
O|
N|Kit spins the bottle. 
O|
S|EXT. BOTTOM OF TRESTLE 
O|
N|The bottle comes to a stop. Kit inspects its position. He does 
N|not appear satisfied, though. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, maybe the slope here is throwing it off some. We ought to 
U|find a more flat place.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|How about over here?
O|
N|Kit starts toward her, then changes his mind and heads back up 
N,G|the grade, to the tracks. The CAMERA DOLLIES and CRANES with him. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Never mind. It doesn't matter... If I'm worth a damn, I'll pick 
U|the right direction. And if I'm not, well, I don't care. See what 
U|I mean?
O|
C,E|HOLLY (o.c.)
U|No.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I shouldn't expect miracles, should I?
O|
N|He stops at the top of the grade and looks out across the 
N|Badlands. 
O|
C|KIT
U|We'll keep on heading for that mountain... Just remember I said 
U|it wasn't such a hot idea.
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. TRESTLE - DUSK 
O|
N|The Cadillac pulls out from under the trestle and heads down a 
N|dusty road alongside the tracks. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|We took off at sunset, on a line toward the mountains of 
U|Saskatchewan, for Kit a magical land beyond the reach of the law. 
U|He needed me now more than ever, but something had come between 
U|us. I'd stopped even paying attention to him. Instead, I sat in 
U|the car and read a map and spelled out entire sentences with my 
U|tongue on the roof of my mouth, where nobody could read them.
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. MOUNTAIN - DUSK 
O|
N|The Cadillac beads off across the plains toward a distant 
N|mountain. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|That night we moved closer to the border, and clear across the 
U|prairie, at the very edge of the horizon... 
O|
S|EXT. REFINERY FIRES - NIGHT 
O|
N|A refinery fire. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|W could make out the gas fires of the refinery at Missoula, while 
U|to the south...
O|
S|EXT. CITY LIGHTS - NIGHT 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|... we could see the lights of Cheyenne, a city bigger and 
U|grander than I'd ever seen.
O|
S,G|EXT. CADILLAC'S POV ON PRAIRIE 
O|
N|The prairie rushes under the Cadillac's headlights. 
O|
C,E,U|HOLLY (v.o.) I felt all kind of things looking at the lights of 
U|Cheyenne, but most important, I made up my mind to never again 
U|tag around with a hell-bent type, no matter how in love with him 
U|I was. Finally, I found the strength to tell Kit this. I pointed 
U|out that even If we got to the Far North, he still couldn't make 
U|a living.
O|
S|INT. CADILLAC - NIGHT 
O|
N|Kit and Holly's faces are strangely lit by the dash lights. Holly 
N|is absorbed in her map. The radio is on. 
O|
C|KIT
U,P|(responding to v.o.) I could get a job with the Mounties. The 
U|Northwest Mounties ... Hell, I got all the qualifications. I can 
U|ride, shoot and... I don't mind the cold. Fact. I kind of like 
U|the cold.
O|
N|Holly looks up from the map. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|What?
O|
C|KIT
U|Nothing... I was just running off at the mouth... as usual.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I'm sorry. I wasn't listening.
O|
N|Holly lights a cigarette. 
O|
C|KIT
U|I was just talking about going after a job... Hey. don't lip it.
O|
N|He reaches over and takes the cigarette out of her mouth. The 
N,G|CAMERA PANS to her with this gesture. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|You know... they'd probably ask to see your driver's license 
U|before they hired you.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|Well. I'm not going to let that stop me.
O|
N,G|When the CAMERA PANS BACK to him, he is looking at her cigarette. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You smoke Pall Mall?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yeah.
O|
N|He is surprised to discover this. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Nah, you hadn't seen me when I'm going after something, honey. 
U|Tough?
O|
N|He cocks his head just thinking about it. She reaches for the 
N|radio to change the station. He pats her hand away. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey. don't touch that... Nat King Cole.
O|
S|EXT. BADLANDS - NIGHT 
O|
N|They dance in the headlights to the tune of "A Blossom Fell" 
N|coming over the car radio. She is wearing the rich man's jacket. 
N|After a moment Kit whispers in her ear. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Boy, if I could sing a song like that... I mean, if I could sing 
U|a song about the way I feel right now, it'd be a hit.
O|
N|They continue dancing. 
O|
S|EXT. CADILLAC ON PLAINS - DAY 
O|
N,G|Two shots of the Cadillac heading across the Badlands at dawn. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit knew the end was coming. He wondered if he'd hear the doctor 
U|pronounce him dead, or if he'd be able to read what the papers 
U|would say about him,the next day, from the other side. He dreaded 
U|the iea of being shot down alone, he said, without a girl to 
U|scream out his name.
O|
S|INT. CADILLAC - DAWN 
O|
N|Holly yawns with fatigue. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Then, for an instant, the sight of the mountains in the dawn 
U|light got his hopes back up.
O|
N|Kit shakes his head with admiration and wonder. 
O|
C|KIT
U|God, what a sight... Tell me this, honey. What does a little 
U|Texas girl like yourself think of a sight like that?
O|
N|She turns to look at the mountains. 
O|
S,G|EXT. MOUNTAINS - THEIR POV 
O|
N|Mountains in the distance closer now than ever before. 
O|
T|DISSOLVE TO
O|
O|
S|EXT. OIL RIG 
O|
N|The Cadillac drives up to an oil rig. Kit gets out of the car. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Love this air.
O|
N|The ROUGHNECK in charge of the rig steps out of his trailer. It 
N|seems there is nobody else around. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Morning... Say, you got any gas?
O|
C|ROUGHNECK
U|Maybe.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well. I'm sorry. sir. but we've got to ask you for it.
O|
N|Holly walks over to inspect the rig. 
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|See, we're about out... been driving all night. Actually, I don't 
U|even have time to explain it to you.
O|
C,E|ROUGHNECK (o.c.)
U|Well, matter of fact, I don't have any.
O|
C|KIT
U|Just a second now. That's your truck. isn't it?
O|
N|He points to a truck linked to the front of the trailer. Holly 
N|looks idly around. Suddenly her expression changes, for what 
N|reason it is not clear. 
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|You didn't walk out here.
O|
C,E|ROUGHNECK (o.c.)
U|It's mine all right.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, listen. I'm going to swap you my Cadillac.
O|
N|The Roughneck does not understand what Kit is up to. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Now don't worry. You're getting a fair deal. Hell. what's the 
U|blue book value on this thing, Mildred?
O|
N|He slaps the hood of the Cadillac and looks to Holly. She is 
N|still preoccupied with whatever it is she has seen, though. 
O|
C,E|ROUGHNECK (o.c.)
U|Who are you?
O|
C|KIT
U|Name is Carruthers. Believe I shoot people every now and then. 
U|Not that I deserve a medal.
O|
N|Holly signals Kit with a nod at the horizon. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Kit!
O|
N|Kit looks up and sees a helicopter in the distance. He hesitates 
N|a moment, then draws his gun. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Okay, friend. start running.
O|
C,E|ROUGHNECK (o.c.)
U|Just gimme a chance.
O|
C|KIT
U|Git.
O|
N|Holly moves around the side of the rig and sits against a tire. 
N|Kit joins her. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Boy. I had a feeling today was going to be the day... Helicopter.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yeah.
O|
C|KIT
U|He's not coming to take us for a ride. either. Come on, let's 
U|make a run for the car.
O|
N|He starts off in the direction of the car, but Holly calls him 
N|back. 
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I don't want to.
O|
N|Kit looks at her. surprised. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Have you got a better idea?
O|
C|HOLLY
U|I just don't want to go.
O|
C|KIT
U|What?
O|
N|She shrugs. He stares at her for a moment, then bows his head and 
N|walks off a distance. 
O|
C|KIT
U|What is wrong with you, huh?
O|
N|He kicks wildly at dust. 
O|
C|KIT
U|What is the matter with you, huh!?
O|
N|Meanwhile, the helicopter continues its approach. Kit walks in 
N|closer to Holly. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Boy, I don't know what to make of you. People like you.
O|
N|He kicks at the rigs then turns back to face her. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You want a second chance, then listen. Twelve noon the Grand 
U|Coulee Dam, New Year'ss Day. 1964. You meet me there... Now you 
U|got that?
O|
N|She nods, bewildered. The helicopter is landing. He grabs a 
N|discarded fender off the truck, hangs it over his arm like a 
N|shield and runs to the Cadillac, exchanging shots with a State 
N|Trooper who has arrived in the helicopter. The Trooper is hit and 
N|falls. Kit jumps in the car and roars off. Holly surrenders 
N|herself to the pilot of the helicopter and is taken off. 
O|
S|EXT. GAS STATION 
O|
N|Kit pulls into a gas station. He gets out of his car and drums on 
N|the roof. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey, anybody here?
O|
N|An ATTENDANT walks out the door. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi... Say,, you got any shells for a Savage, a .300 Savage?
O|
C|ATTENDANT
U|Nope.
O|
C|KIT
U|Damn. I pissed all mine away shooting up bottles... You want to 
U|fill her up for me? Please?
O|
N|The Attendant nods. Kit pulls Holly's suitcase out of the back 
N|seat and walks over to a trash can at the edge of the apron. Kit 
N|throws Holly's things methodically into the trash can. After a 
N|while he comes upon a book. He opens the book and looks through 
N|it, then turns to find the Attendant staring at him. As he gets 
N|to his feet. he nods at the trash can. 
O|
C|KIT
U|If you want any of that junk, it's yours.
O|
N|Meanwhile, a police car approaches the station. Kit sees it at 
N|about the same time the police see him. He throws his book in the 
N|car and roars out of the station. The police car goes into a 
N|bootlegger's turn and takes off after him. 
O|
S,G|CHASE - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|Kit heads down the highway. After a while he turns off onto a 
N|dirt road. The police keep up with him.. He flips on the radio 
N|and fires back at them. The dust is so heavy that they have a 
N|hard time just keeping track of him. He checks his own appearance 
N|and general demeanor in the rear view mirror, then swerves off 
N|the road onto the prairie. The police manage to keep up. Together 
N|they roar through a herd of cows and barb wire fence. The police 
N|car slams into Kit, trying to slow him down. Kit slams back into 
N|the police car. They return to the road. Kit makes a ninety-
N|degree turn but the police car loses traction and rolls onto its 
N|side, then back onto four wheels again, stalled. Kit sees that he 
N|has lost them. He checks himself out in the mirror again, puts on 
N|the rich man's hat and skids to a stop. The police car gets back 
N|underway. During these last few moments Holly is heard on VOICE 
N|OVER. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Often I've wondered what was going through Kit's head before they 
U|got him and why he didn't make a run for it while he still had 
U|the chance. Did he figure theyld just catch up with him the next 
U|day? Was it despair? He claimed to having a flat tire, but the 
U|way he carried on about it, I suspect this is false.
O|
S|EXT. ROAD 
O|
N|Kit shoots a hole in the tire of the Cadillac, then climbs up on 
N,G|the hood. We see how far behind the police are. He checks his 
N|pulse and jumps back down. He scurries around collecting rocks 
N|from the shoulder as the police approach from the distance. He 
N|makes a stack of the rocks to mark the site of his capture for 
N|posterity, finishing just as the police car skids to a stop. Kit 
N|throws up his hands and walks forward to meet them. Their guns 
N|are drawn. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hi.
O|
C|SHERIFF
U|Hold it right there.
O|
C|KIT
U|I could've held off an army if I could've gotten behind a rock in 
U|the mountains.
O|
C,E|DEPUTY (o.c.)
U|Oh yeah?
O|
C,P|KIT (nodding)
U|Long as my ammo held out... Right there's where you caught me.
O|
N|He nods at the stack of rocks. The Sheriff frisks him as the 
N|Deputy covers. 
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|We did it, Ray.
O|
C|KIT
U|You better not leave that Cadillac sitting out here.
O|
N|The Sheriff takes out a pair of handcuffs. 
O|
C|SHERIFF
U|Put your hands in back of your head.
O|
N|The Sheriff claps the handcuffs on one of Kit's wrists. 
O|
C|SHERIFF
U|Now put the other one on yourself.
O|
N|Kit does as the Sheriff asks. The Deputy holsters his pistol and 
N|walks forward to inspect their prize. He and KIT exchange 
N|glances. 
O|
C,P|DEPUTY (to Sheriff)
U|Hell, he ain't no bigger than I am.
O|
N|The Sheriff sticks his pistol in Kit's ear and leads him off 
N|toward the police car. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You're going to give me a cauliflower ear, Sheriff.
O|
N|The Sheriff gives Kit a shove. 
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|He should've thought about that before he got caught... Shouldn't 
U|he?
O|
N|The Deputy draws his pistol and fires a shot into the neighboring 
N|field. Kit is startled. The Deputy makes the same motion, this 
N|time without firing. 
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Hup!
O|
S|EXT. HIGHWAY 
O|
N|The police car sails down an open stretch of highway. 
O|
S|INT. POLICE CAR 
O|
N|Kit thinks for a while, then turns to the Sheriff, who is 
N|driving. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Think I'll take the juice?
O|
C|SHERIFF
U|Beats me.
O|
N|The Deputy, fascinated, watches all this from the back seat. Kit 
N|turns to him. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Say, what kind of rifle was that you were shooting at me?
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Thirty aught six.
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|You ever had to open it up like that before?
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Nope.
O|
N|Kit turns back around. 
O|
C,P|KIT (reassuringly)
U|Well, you boys have performed like a couple of heroes... And 
U|don't think I'm not going to pass it around when we get to town.
O|
N|The Sheriff lifts the hat off Kit's head and tosses it out the 
N|window. It rolls to a stop on the highway behind them. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You tossed my hat out the window.
O|
C,E|SHERIFF (o.c.)
U|Wanta sue me?
O|
C|KIT
U|No.
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Kit... Kit, I've got a question for you.
O|
C|KIT
U|Mmmmm.
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|You like people?
O|
C|KIT
U|They're okay.
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Then why'd you do it?
O|
C|KIT
U|I don't know. Always wanted to be a criminal, I guess. Just not 
U|this big a one... Takes all kinds though.
O|
N|Kit gets a smile from the Deputy. The Deputy turns to the 
N|Sheriff. 
O|
C|DEPUTY
U,P|You know who that sombitch looks like? (no reply) You know, don't 
U|you?
O|
N|Kit is anxious to hear the answer. 
O|
C,E|SHERIFF (o.c.)
U|No.
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|I'll kiss your ass if he don't look like James Dean.
O|
N|Kit drops his eyes and turns away, deeply gratified by this 
N|remark. The Sheriff smiles at the Deputy in the rear mirror. The 
N|car roars past a freight train. 
O|
S|INT. AIRPORT HANGAR 
O|
N|Kit stands on the wing of a light plane, inside a large hangar at 
N|the local airport. He is shackled in a leather transfer vest. He 
N|takes souvenirs out of his pockets and tosses them out to a crowd 
N|of Montana State Troopers and National Guardsmen milling around 
N|on the tarmac ground below him. 
O|
C|MECHANIC
U|How you doing there, Kit?
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|Fine as can be expected. Just locked up, can't move.
O|
C,E|MECHANIC (o.c.)
U|That's a nice little coat you got on there.
O|
C|KIT
U|You think?... it's leather.
O|
N|He takes out his cigarette lighter. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey, now here's a real prize. I must've had this about ten years. 
U|Who's going to get it?
O|
C,E|VOICE (o.c.)
U|Gimme that sombitch.
O|
C|KIT
U|There you go.
O|
C,E|SECOND VOICE (o.c.)
U|Where you from?
O|
C|KIT
U|South Dakota. Where you from?
O|
C,E|SECOND VOICE (o.c.)
U|Here.
O|
C|KIT
U|Want a comb?
O|
N|He takes a comb out of his pocket and pitches it down to 
N|somebody. He ignores most of the questions coming at him. 
O|
C,E|THIRD VOICE (o.c.)
U|Joni James is from South Dakota.
O|
C|KIT
U|Yeah, she got out of there, too.
O|
C,E|FOURTH VOICE (o.c.)
U|You ever been to Kansas?
O|
C,E|FIFTH VOICE (o.c.)
U|Hey, Kit. whot's your favorite singer?
O|
C|KIT
U|Eddie Fisher. Who's yours?
O|
C,E|FIFTH VOICE (o.c.)
U|Eddie Fisher.
O|
C|KIT
U|Damn! Want a pen?
O|
N,G|He pitches the man a ballpoint. The CAMERA MOVES BACK to a WIDE 
N,G|SHOT of the whole scene. 
O|
C,E|SIXTH VOICE (o.c.)
U|How old are you?
O|
C|KIT
U|Don't you read the papers?
O|
C,E|SIXTH VOICE (.o.c.)
U|You ever been married?
O|
C|KIT
U|No sir, I hadn't.
O|
S,G|EXT. HANGAR - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N,G|Various SHOTS of the National Guardsmen dozing on the pavement. 
N,G|playing cards beside the hangar, a little bored. Also a shot of 
N,G|Holly with the Sheriff and Deputy. 
O|
C,E|SEVENTH VOICE (o.c.)
U|Hey, Kit, you afraid of death?
O|
C,E|KIT (o.c.)
U|I hadn't thought about it much.
O|
S|INT. HANGAR 
O|
N|The Deputy walks up to Kit with some legal papers. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Hey, listen, Tom, I don't mean to tell you how to run your show 
U|here but these cuffs are pinching. What do you say now? 
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|I need to get your signature on some papers here, Kit.
O|
C|KIT
U|Well, I've got to read them first. Suppose I could get a Coke 
U|while I do?
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Sure thing. Come on.
O|
N|He gives Kit the papers and leads him out of the hangar. Kit 
N|turns back to the crowd inside. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You boys keep out of trouble.
O|
C|GUARDSMAN
U|Don't worry about us.
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Holly's over here, Kit. if you want to see her.
O|
C|KIT
U|Sure.
O|
S|EXT. POLICE CAR 
O|
N|Holly leans against the fender of the police car. She wears 
N|handcuffs. Kit turns to a State Trooper who holds him by a 
N|tether. 
O|
C|KIT
U|You want to give me a little slack here?
O|
N|The Trooper pays out the slack. Kit leans against the car. He and 
N|Holly exchange looks. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Don't worry now.. I'm going to get you off these charges... 
U|There's a whole lot of other boys out there. You're going to have 
U|a lot of fun.
O|
N|Holly looks down. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Boy, we rang the bells, didn't we?
O|
N|Holly does not reply. 
O|
C|KIT
U|I'll say one thing, though... That guy with the deaf maid? He's 
U|just lucky he's not dead, too.
O|
N|Holly does not appreciate the humor of this. Kit, chastened, 
N|turns serious. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Course it's too bad about your dad.
O|
C|HOLLY
U|Yeah.
O|
C|KIT
U|We're going to have to sit down, and talk about that sometime.
O|
N|He looks up at the SOUND of an incoming plane. 
O|
S,G|EXT. AIRPORT - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|The plane that will take them back to South Dakota, a B-25, comes 
N|in for a landing. The mass of Troopers and National Guardsmen, 
N|with Kit and Holly, walks out to meet it. A jeep leads it down 
N|the taxiway. A civilian bystander points Kit and Holly out to a 
N|young boy he holds in his arms. 
O|
S|EXT. PLANE 
O|
N|Kit signs his extradition papers on the back of the Chief 
N|Trooper, gives them to the Deputy and shakes hands with the 
N|Chief. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Thank you very much, Chief Sims. Sorry if I caused you any 
U|inconvenience.
O|
C|CHIEF
U|You didn't cause me any trouble.
O|
C|KIT
U|Thanks anyway.
O|
N|He turns to shake hands with the Deputy. The planets engines whir 
N|to life. 
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Well, Kit...
O|
C|KIT
U|Tom...
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|Good luck to you.
O|
C|KIT
U|Thanks.
O|
C|DEPUTY
U|I mean it.
O|
C|KIT
U|I know you do. Good luck to you, too.
O|
C|TROOPER
U|Now we go.
O|
N|The Trooper pulls him toward the plane. Kit calls Holly. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Come on, Red.
O|
S,G|EXT. RUNWAY - SERIES OF ANGLES 
O|
N|The props start turning. The crowd waves goodbye as the boarding 
N|door is closed. The wheels turn toward the sun. The Guardsmen 
N|race back to their trucks. A mailman carries off the mail bags he 
N|collected from the plane. The plane taxies down the runway and 
N|lifts into the air. 
O|
C,E|HOLLY (v.o.)
U|Kit and I were taken back to South Dakota. They kept him in 
U|solitary, so he didn't have a chance to get acquainted with the 
U|other inmates, though he was sure they'd like him, especially the 
U|murderers. Myself, I got off with probation and a lot of nasty 
U|looks. Later I married the son of the lawyer who defended me. 
U|(pause) Kit went to sleep in the courtroom while his confession 
U|was being read, and he was sentenced to die in the electric 
U|chair. On a warm spring night, six months later, after donating 
U|his body to science, he did. 
O|
N,G,T|With Holly's last words we CUT INSIDE the cabin of the plane. 
O|
S|INT. CABIN 
O|
N|Kit sits next to the Trooper at the back of the plane. Holly sits 
N|opposite them, with a Detective behind her. Kit's attention is 
N|fixed on the military hat in the Trooper's lap. 
O|
C|KIT
U|Sir... Where'd you get that hat?
O|
C|TROOPER
U|State.
O|
C|KIT
U|Boy, I'd like to buy me one of those.
O|
N|The Trooper smiles. Holly looks up. 
O|
C|TROOPER
U|You're quite an individual, Kit.
O|
C|KIT
U|Think they'll take that into consideration?
O|
N|The Trooper smiles and looks out the window. Kit looks to Holly, 
N|to see if she has taken this remark in the proper spirit. She 
N|smiles and looks out the window. 
O|
S,G|EXT. CLOUDS - HOLLY'S POV - DUSK 
O|
N|An unbroken sea of clouds stretches out before her. 
O|
T|FADE OUT. 
O|
M|END CREDITS OVER BLACK. 
O|
M|THE END
O|
